# ============================================================================
# FlowConjugateHeat / a shock reflecting from a conducting wall heats it  (Hydro2, ablation.conduction = 1, no ablation)
#
# Gas at rest (rho 1, p 1, T 1; gamma 1.4, cp 3.5; mu 1e-3, Pr 0.72 -> k = 4.861e-3) in x < 0.8, conducting solid
# (rho_s cp_s = 14, k_s = 1.944e-2, T = 1) in x > 0.8.  A Mach 1.2 shock (S = 1.4199; behind it rho 1.3416,
# u 0.3615, p 1.5133, T 1.1280) starts at x = 0.5, reaches the wall at t_r = 0.2113 and reflects, leaving
# gas at rest at p5 = 2.2371, T5 = 1.2635 against the wall at T = 1.
# From then on: a gas half-space at CONSTANT PRESSURE p5 (density ~ 1/T, the gas moves toward the wall as it cools)
# against a solid half-space.  Similarity solution in the mass coordinate psi = int rho dx, eta = psi/sqrt(t - t_r):
#     -(eta/2) dT/d eta = (k p5/(cp R)) d/d eta((1/T) dT/d eta),   T(inf) = T5,
# coupled to the error-function profile in the solid by a constant surface temperature T_i and equal fluxes
#     q_w sqrt(t - t_r) = e_s (T_i - T_s0)/sqrt(pi),      e_s = sqrt(k_s rho_s cp_s);
# solved by shooting in the check script (the linearised value is T_i = (e_5 T5 + e_s T_s0)/(e_5 + e_s)).
# Checks the dynamic and the thermal coupling together: shock arrival time, reflected state, surface temperature,
# wall heat flux ~ 1/sqrt(t - t_r), temperature profiles on both sides.
#
# Run (from bin/): mpirun -np 4 ./hydro2-2d-g++ ../tests/FlowConjugateHeat/input_1D_ShockHeatedWall plot_file=<dir>/output
# Check:           python3 ../tests/FlowConjugateHeat/reference/shock_heated_wall_check.py <dir> [<dir> ...]
